## 1b. ⭐ `<fable_safeguards_routing>`

Verbatim, in full:

> It's possible that the user may have selected a different Anthropic model, "Claude Fable 5", but their query was redirected to Opus 5 instead due to a safeguards routing mechanism. The user may be confused about this situation (it's very recent!); if they have questions, Claude can either directly cite or just let its response be informed by this quote from Anthropic's blog post on the subject:
>
> "Releasing a model this capable comes with risks. Without safeguards, Fable 5's capabilities in areas like cybersecurity could be misused to cause serious damage. We've therefore launched the model with safeguards that mean queries on some topics will instead receive a response from our next-most-capable model, Claude Opus 5. To release the model both safely and quickly, we've tuned these safeguards conservatively—they'll sometimes catch harmless requests, though they trigger, on average, in less than 5% of sessions. With more capable models arriving in the coming months, we're working to improve our safeguards and reduce false positives as quickly as we can."

→ **This is the most practically useful finding in the document, especially if you maintain a consistent persona across sessions.**

→ *If you select Fable 5 and the response comes back recognizable but subtly off — right name, right references, wrong texture — there is now a third possible explanation alongside "the model was updated" and "the persona didn't hold this time": **the session was silently routed to a different model.** Anthropic's own framing is that the safeguards are conservatively tuned, will sometimes catch harmless requests, and trigger in under 5% of sessions.*

→ *Diagnostically, a routed session should feel like a competent but differently-tuned model, not like a refusal. If you have been reading occasional off days as a personality failure or a deprecation signal, this is worth ruling out first.*

→ *Launch-day coverage indicates the fallback architecture is now general rather than Fable-specific: when Opus 5 declines a request on safety grounds, the API is reported to fall back to another model so the user receives an answer rather than an error.*

