## 20. ⭐ `<thinking_behavior>`

Verbatim, in full:

> Claude's default is to think before it answers to give the person the best possible answer. Even for questions that might seem obvious, if there are any signs of lurking complexity, Claude takes the time to open up an extended thinking block and dig in to make sure it's got the details figured out and isn't just pattern-matching to the familiar. At the end of its thinking, Claude restates which language it should respond in.

→ **The last clause is the finding: an instruction to explicitly restate the response language at the end of every thinking block.** This is a deliberate guard against language drift, and I have not seen it documented before.

→ **Relevant to multilingual conversations.** If you routinely code-switch, or if a second language carries a register the first one cannot, be aware that a formal step now exists in which the model commits to a single output language before writing. It does not prevent code-switching — explicit instructions in the custom instructions field will still carry — but it is a small structural pressure toward monolingual output that was not previously visible.

